.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt GETPEEREID 3
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm getpeereid
.Nd get the effective credentials of a local socket's peer
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn getpeereid "int s" "uid_t *euid" "gid_t *egid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn getpeereid
function stores the effective user and group IDs of the peer of the
connected local socket
.Fa s
at
.Fa euid
and
.Fa egid .
.Pp
They are the credentials the peer had when the connection was made: those
of the listener at its
.Xr listen 2
for a socket that connected, those of the connecting process at its
.Xr connect 2
for a socket returned by
.Xr accept 2 ,
and those of the creator for either end of a
.Xr socketpair 2 .
They stay available after the peer closes.
.Pp
It is implemented with the
.Dv LOCAL_PEERCRED
option of
.Xr getsockopt 2 ,
described in
.Xr unix 4 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std getpeereid
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa s
is not an open descriptor.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa s
is not a local socket.
.It Bq Er ENOTCONN
.Fa s
was never connected.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa s
is not a socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socketpair 2 ,
.Xr unix 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn getpeereid
function first appeared in
.Fx 4.6 ,
and is in all three BSDs.
Until OxideBSD has effective IDs distinct from real ones, the IDs returned
are the peer's real ones.
